namespace KlikerBlocks;

// Reviewed, offline English help. These texts deliberately keep command names and limits intact.
internal static class EnglishHelp
{
    public static string Overview => "Kliker Blocks " + AppIdentity.VersionLabel + " is a public beta visual Windows scenario editor. At first launch, choose English or Russian before the editor and demo open; later change it in Languages. Connect blocks for conditions, loops, input, files and screenshots. Run from the editor, compact panel or tray. Search Help by block, function or parameter; each block has a copyable example graph. Run File offers an installed-program list and file browser. The demo follows the interface language and does not require translation models. Translation blocks need a configured service or optional offline models installed after confirmation. The release includes separate x64 and x86 installers and portable builds. Choose x64 on 64-bit Windows, or x86 on 32-bit Windows. Scenario → Start with Windows toggles autostart for your Windows account; the installer also offers this option. Pin the running app through the Windows taskbar context menu.";

    public static string? Section(string title) => title switch
    {
        "Точки и маршруты связей" => "In Block appearance, customize every input and named output port separately: angle, shape (circle, square, diamond or triangle), size (6–36), fill and opacity. Ports stay on the block outline. Port labels are hidden on blocks; hover a port to see its name, which disappears when the pointer leaves. A red square “II” badge marks a breakpoint; enable it in block properties to pause the scenario before that block runs. Hover the badge for a tooltip. A breakpoint is not a connector. A connection's end marker size (4–48) is independent of line width. Set straight leads from the output and before the input separately (0–200). Set the first output corner radius and last input corner radius independently (0–200); zero keeps a sharp turn. The actual radius is limited by adjacent segments and reduced when a rounded corner would overlap a block. A connecting segment runs between the corners. With both leads zero and a clear path, the connection can be straight; otherwise it routes around blocks. Styles are saved with the graph. In graph code, breakpoint true pauses before a block.",
        "Отладка сценария" => "Enable Breakpoint before block in block properties. A run stops before that block, highlights it, and shows current variables in the debugger. Use Next block to execute one step or Continue to resume normally. The usual Stop command ends the whole scenario. Variables may contain private data; consider this when sharing screenshots.",
        "Поддержать проект" => "Supporting development is voluntary and not required to use the app. The Support the project menu shows five author-supplied QR codes: USDT on TON, TRC20, ERC20 and Solana, plus Sberbank. For USDT select the exact network supported by your wallet. Verify the recipient details decoded from any QR before sending; incorrect transactions generally cannot be reversed. The same QR codes are published on the Kliker Blocks website.",
        "Панель, пауза и трей" => "Closing the editor opens the compact Kliker Blocks panel. Run, pause, resume or stop the current scenario there, and reopen the editor. Closing the panel hides it in the system tray; use Exit Kliker Blocks in the panel or tray menu to quit. Each instance has its own tray icon: green means running, yellow paused, gray ready. Pausing is cooperative: an action already in progress finishes before execution waits. Kliker Blocks releases held keys and buttons at a safe pause boundary.",
        "Поделиться графом" => "Click Share graph in the editor. Review the code: block settings may contain paths, text and other personal details. A small graph opens a prefilled GitHub discussion. A large graph copies its full code to the clipboard so you can paste it into the discussion body. Select the User graphs category and confirm publication in your browser. Nothing is posted without confirmation. The graph appears on the website after synchronization. Review file, window and network actions before running another user's graph.",
        "Обработка ошибки блока" => "For each executable block choose what happens on failure: stop the scenario, skip to the Next output, or jump to a selected block. Set 0–10 retries and a 0–60000 ms delay before each retry. After a handled error its text is available in ${_last_error} and written to the journal. Scenario cancellation and subroutine return are not block errors. Example: configure CSV reading for two retries with a 500 ms delay, then jump to a Log block that writes ${_last_error}.",
        "Запуск и остановка" => "A scenario starts at its single Start block. Select one pass or repeat from the beginning until stopped. Run from the Scenario menu, compact panel, tray, or Ctrl+Alt+F6. Stop there or with Ctrl+Alt+F7; pause from the menu, panel or tray. An unconnected output ends its branch. A backward connection creates a loop.",
        "Автозапуск, расписание и обновления" => "Use Scenario → Scenario startup and schedules to select a saved .klb, enable Kliker Blocks at Windows sign-in, and start that scenario immediately or after a 0–3600 second delay. Opening the app manually does not run the startup scenario. Scheduled starts accept a date and time to the second, once or daily. Windows Task Scheduler launches a separate app instance per trigger, so several scenarios can run at once. The user must be signed in. Enable, disable or remove tasks in the same window. Windows decides how missed tasks run after a computer was off.\n\nUse Help → Check for updates to query public GitHub releases. Download the installer for the current architecture; the app verifies its size and SHA-256 against GitHub data, backs up the open graph under AppData\\Local\\KLIKER Blocks\\GraphBackups, then starts the installer. Your .klb files and settings are outside the release payload and are not deleted. New graphs save to Documents\\Kliker Blocks by default. File → Check graph compatibility reads a .klb without changing it. Graph format v1 remains compatible; any future migration must preserve the original and a backup.",
        "Выделение тонких связей" => "Hover over a connection. The line turns blue and a circular handle appears at its midpoint. Click the line or handle to edit connection properties on the right. An invisible click area extends around thin lines and adapts to zoom and width; blocks still take click priority. Press Delete to remove the selected connection.",
        "Демонстрация Что я умею" => "The What I can do menu opens an interactive graph. It types and verifies text in Notepad, asks questions, draws your chosen shape in Paint, finds a selected color, and reports the results. Translation is deliberately excluded because the lightweight edition does not bundle Python or language models. On a retry, the demo undoes earlier strokes and selects the drawing tool again. If the old Windows Beep API is unavailable, the sound-signal block uses normal Windows audio. Each new Kliker Blocks version offers the demo again; desktop actions still require consent. Esc or Stop interrupts later actions but leaves opened windows and files. The clipboard changes. Do not run it over sensitive content.",
        "Окна и точность демонстрации" => "When space allows, Notepad is placed left of the graph. The entire document is copied and verified, not just a visually wrapped line. Missed characters are corrected through the clipboard and verified again. Paint starts with a blank canvas; Kliker Blocks locates the pencil and red color, measures the visible white area and draws inside it with mouse input. If the interface cannot be recognized or no strokes appear, the demo reports an error. Classic Paint was not available on the test computer for a live integration test.",
        "Визуальные блоки и связи" => "The left palette shows section headings that expand to block lists; the first section is open when the editor starts. Block names use rounded cards. The palette has a minimum width to keep labels readable. Use Hide blocks in the editor toolbar to collapse it to the left, and Show blocks to reopen it. Search expands matching blocks. Add blocks from the Blocks menu or left palette. Left-click empty canvas to set a green insertion point. The next new or pasted block goes there; without a point, new blocks follow the usual sequence. If blocks are selected, left-click empty canvas to center the selection there while preserving their spacing. Right-click empty canvas clears both selection and insertion point. Every input and named output stays on the block outline, and every output can connect to another block input. Configure each port separately. Port labels are hidden on the block; hover a port to see its tooltip, which disappears when the pointer leaves. A breakpoint uses a separate square pause badge and is not a connector. Connections route around other blocks and have an arrow by default. Click a connection to choose its end marker, solid or patterned line, color, opacity and width. In Block appearance, choose a rounded rectangle, rectangle, square, circle or diamond, plus fill and border colors and opacity. Set the input and each output position by angle on the outline (0° right, 90° down, 180° left, 270° up), or click the preview. Apply a style to one block or save it as the default for that block type across projects. Individual styles and port positions are saved with the project. Drag empty canvas to pan; Shift+left drag selects an area. Ctrl+C/V copies and pastes blocks; Delete removes a block or link. Ctrl+wheel zooms. The arrow icon in the editor toolbar opens the graph in a separate window. The arrow beside Execution journal opens its live log in a separate window. Edit the selected block on the right.",
        "Размер и заголовок блока" => "Open Block appearance in the selected block properties. Choose a width from 196 to 480 units and a title font size from 7 to 16 points. Automatically shrink the title to fit is enabled by default; when needed, it reduces the title down to a readable minimum. Increase the width for more space. When a breakpoint is enabled, the title starts after its II badge. Width and title settings are saved with this block in the project.",
        "Текстовый код Kliker Blocks" => "Use Blocks / Kliker Blocks code above the canvas to edit the same graph as text, including cycles and parallel links. Syntax: block <label> <type> <X> <Y> [id <GUID>], followed by value \"Field\" = \"Value\" and link \"Output\" -> <label> or none, then end. A local block style appears as appearance = {...}; an output connection style appears as style \"Output\" = {...}. Both use JSON and are easiest to edit in properties first. The default style for each block type is stored in app settings. Values are JSON strings: a real line break inside a field appears as \\n in graph code. Windows may create \\r\\n, which Kliker Blocks normalizes to \\n. A backslash is doubled in code: the template escape \\& in properties appears as \\\\& in code. trace true enables per-block journal entries. A new block can omit id; keep existing GUIDs when editing. Apply code validates the entire text before changing the graph. Each block's help page offers a copyable example graph. Save your project first, paste the example in code mode, apply it and run once. Some examples require the stated file, window or service.",
        "Переменные и массивы" => "User variable and array names contain only A–Z, a–z, 0–9 and _. The first character must be a letter or _: counter, total_2, _flag are valid; 2total and my-value are not. Names are case-insensitive. Check names with Languages → Check project variable names.\n\nSet variable creates or replaces a value; Define variable only creates it if missing. Math variable changes a numeric value, starting from 0 if missing. If variable selects Yes/No. Undefine also removes the array of the same name. Values are stored as strings; arithmetic needs numeric input.\n\nUse ${counter} to interpolate into a field. A field beginning with = is evaluated as an expression, for example =($counter+2)*3 or =$counter>=10 and $enabled. Use $counter, not ${counter}, inside expressions. Array items are $items[0]; arrsize($items) returns size and arrpop($items) removes the last item. Indices start at 0.\n\nVariables are shared by blocks, subroutines and parallel branches within one run. They persist between passes of a repeating scenario but reset on a new run; current values are not saved in .klb. Parallel writes to the same name have no defined order. System values include $_xmouse, $_ymouse, $_time_h, $_time_m, $_time_s, $_date_y, $_date_m, $_date_d, $_time_str, $_date_str, $_time_t, $_ms, $_xmin, $_ymin, $_xmax and $_ymax. Do not log secrets.",
        "Журнал и запись в файл" => "Each block has a Log actions checkbox. When enabled, completion adds its name, short ID and chosen output to the execution journal; errors are logged too. Explicit messages and Kliker Blocks diagnostics appear regardless. In graph code use trace true.\n\nScenario → Write journal to text file records the full current run, including diagnostics and selected block actions. Enter a .txt/.log filename without a path. The file is created at run start beside a saved .klb, or beside Kliker.Blocks.exe for an unsaved project; its full path is shown in settings and the on-screen journal. UTF-8 output is appended across runs, up to 8 MiB. This project setting does not clear the file when the on-screen journal is cleared. The separate Log file block/#logfile creates its file when reached but writes explicit scenario messages only. Avoid recording passwords or tokens.",
        "Снимки и образы" => "The Capture menu saves points, screen regions and templates. The Graph library menu opens one of seven examples in a working folder under Documents\\Kliker Blocks\\Graph Library, with sample files and English instructions. Select text and press Ctrl+Alt+T to open the screen menu. It sends Ctrl+C and changes the clipboard; some applications do not allow copying. Speak the selection with a Windows voice, save it as TXT, or create a note. Choose “Select screen area…” and draw a rectangle. The menu reopens and shows the saved PNG path. Escape returns to the menu without taking a new screenshot. OCR needs Tesseract and eng+rus language data. Screen menu settings configure the hotkey, folder and editable .klb graphs; the examples button creates sample graphs. A graph receives selected_text, screenshot_path and output_folder. Use Stop to cancel it. Notes store text and one image in a .klnote file, opened from Capture. Select text and use the formatting icon to change font, size, color, bold, italic or underline. Ctrl+plus and Ctrl+minus zoom the image; drag it to reposition it. Formatting and image view are saved in the note. Click a note to show its title and icon controls; hover over an icon for its tooltip. The header hides after three seconds of inactivity. Drag the title to move the note, use an edge or the lower-right grip to resize the window, and drag the blue divider to resize its text and image sections. The image stretches to fill its section by default; use the aspect icon to preserve its proportions. Separate icons remove and restore the sections; the disk icon saves changes.",
        "Запись макросов" => "The Macro menu offers full input recording, clicks with image templates, and clicks on Windows UI Automation elements. In the semantic mode, captured buttons become Wait for element and Element action blocks; controls with no usable selector fall back to coordinates. The recorder panel can pause, resume and stop. Save the result in the library and convert it to editable blocks. Review recorded selectors before replay because an app can change its accessible names.",
        "Слежение за курсором" => "Watch cursor can start immediately, toggle with a key, or work while a key is held. It may additionally require the left, right or middle mouse button or another key. Configure anchor, movement threshold, return target, polling interval and delay. Applications may handle mouse input differently; bypassing protection is not supported.",
        "Индикатор сценария" => "The always-on-top indicator shows scenario and tracking states. Drag it with the left mouse button; save the project to retain its position. In the Scenario menu choose position, shape, size, colors or images for each state, captions and show/hide hotkey. Each instance has its own indicator and project settings.",
        "Профили и назначения мыши" => "Open Mouse mappings → Profiles, layers and buttons. Create a profile and enable mappings. Profiles are checked top to bottom; All windows is the fallback. Match the active or hovered window by process, title, class or a rectangle relative to its top-left corner. Empty match fields impose no restriction. Each profile has 1–10 layers. Add mappings from a button/wheel event and trigger mode to an action. Supported triggers include press, release, hold, repeat, toggle, chord, short press and up to four long-press thresholds. Key actions accept Ctrl+C or sequences such as Ctrl+C, 100ms, Ctrl+V. Block original suppresses the source event; the wheel supports individual scroll events only. Ctrl+Alt+F8 toggles mappings; holding Ctrl+Alt temporarily passes the original mouse input through. Mappings pause over Kliker Blocks windows. Two instances can run scenarios concurrently, but enable mouse remapping in only one to avoid duplicate actions. Some applications may ignore synthetic Windows input.",
        "Перевод текста и языковые модели" => "Languages → Interface language and translation models selects RU/EN/FR/ES/ZH, a Python executable with argostranslate, and an optional LibreTranslate URL. Changing language recreates the interface automatically, retaining unsaved project data in memory. Translate text supports LibreTranslate /translate (source auto is allowed) or Local Argos (explicit source language and installed model required). Detect language uses /detect; If translation available uses /languages. These two blocks require a running LibreTranslate server. Translate array processes array entries through the chosen engine. ${source_text} substitutes a variable. A remote service receives the text you send to it. Detailed help in EN is built in; FR/ES/ZH require a local ru → target model for full topic text. Translation quality depends on the model; a custom terminology dictionary is not yet provided.",
        "Установка модели Argos" => "1. Install a Python version supported by argostranslate, then run python -m pip install argostranslate in a terminal. If Python 3.14 dependencies fail, try a separate Python 3.11 or 3.12.\n\n2. In Languages → Interface language and translation models enter that same python.exe path. Click Check Argos. A missing-module error means argostranslate was installed into another Python.\n\n3. Download a .argosmodel with the correct direction. ru → en translates Russian to English; ar → en translates Arabic to English and does not substitute for ru → en. Reverse translation needs a separate package.\n\n4. Click Install .argosmodel…, select the downloaded file, and check the installed-model list. In the Translate text block choose Local Argos and the source/target codes. FR/ES/ZH help translation needs ru → the selected interface language.\n\nThe LibreTranslate check contacts a separate server, initially 127.0.0.1:5000; Kliker Blocks does not start it. A connection error means no server is listening there. Local Argos does not need that server. Detect language and If translation available currently do require LibreTranslate. Install models only from trusted sources.",
        "Возможности компьютера и голоса" => "At first launch, Kliker Blocks saves a local inventory to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KLIKER Blocks\\computer-capabilities.json: Windows and .NET versions, architecture, UI and keyboard languages, installed voices and display bounds. If that folder is not writable, the file is saved beside Kliker.Blocks.exe; the window shows the actual path. It does not record the user name, document paths or installed programs and does not upload the file. Open Languages → Computer and voices to inspect or refresh it after adding a voice or display. Voices are not downloaded automatically. For Speak text, leave Voice blank: Russian and Chinese script select matching voices, other text uses the system default. Russian text needs a ru voice, such as Irina or Pavel; English Zira cannot read it properly. Add a missing voice in Windows language settings. Stopping the scenario interrupts speech playback.",
        "Коды клавиш VK (1–255)" => "Windows virtual-key (VK) codes are shown as decimal, hexadecimal and name. Enter the decimal number in numeric block fields, or the number/short name in text key fields. Use the capture button in block settings to assign a key without looking up its code. Left mouse=1, right mouse=2, middle mouse=4. Some codes are unassigned; OEM keys depend on keyboard layout.\n\n" +
            string.Join("\n", VirtualKeyCatalog.AllCodes().Select(item => $"{item.Code,3}   0x{item.Code:X2}   {VirtualKeyCatalog.DescribeEnglish(item.Code)}")),
        _ => null
    };

    public static string? Block(string kind) => kind switch
    {
        "Note" => "Manages screen notes. Create opens a new window; Open loads a .klnote file. Set an ID or leave it blank to generate one; the result variable receives the ID. Change, Read, Save, Minimize, Restore and Close address an open note by ID, shown in its title; Read returns its text. Change file edits a saved note without showing a window and returns its path; Read file returns its saved text. Choose whether to leave, replace or append text and whether to leave, load or remove an image. Blank X/Y and size fields keep their current values. Saving to a specified path replaces an existing file. Closing a modified note asks whether to save it. Example: create note1 with text, then minimize it with another Note block using ID note1.",
        "Start" => "Entry point. A project needs exactly one Start block. Connect Next to the first action.",
        "ShowcaseStep" => "Internal step of the What I can do demo graph. Action selects a built-in demo operation; Caption labels the node. Running an exported graph requires consent. Stop interrupts pending actions, but opened windows and files remain and clipboard content may have changed. Requires a Kliker Blocks build that supports this demo.",
        "Description" => "Background label for a group of blocks. It does not execute and has no connectors. Drag its top bar or border to move it, or its lower-right corner to resize it. Set caption, size, #RRGGBB color and opacity (0–100%) in properties. Blocks inside are not attached to it.",
        "WatchCursor" => "Returns the system cursor when it moves from its anchor. Activation can be immediate, toggled, or active while a key is held; a mouse button can be required too. Assign keys by pressing them in the capture dialog. The indicator is red while waiting and green when toggled on. Example: F9 plus left mouse button. Duration 0 means until stopped in one-pass mode, or one check per pass in repeating mode.",
        "MoveCursor" => "Moves the system cursor once using SetCursorPos: to the monitor center, absolute X/Y, an offset from its current position, or an offset from the monitor center. Offsets can be negative. For applications reading relative motion, use Move mouse relatively instead.",
        "MoveRelative" => "Sends a relative movement with Windows SendInput. Positive X moves right; positive Y moves down. Delay postpones the start. Duration 0 or one step sends the whole motion at once; multiple steps distribute the exact total ΔX/ΔY across the duration. For one action per key press, use If key down → On new press. For repeated motion while held, choose While held and Repeat until stopped. A second block with negative deltas can reverse the motion after a Wait. This does not measure or set a game's camera position. Stop with Ctrl+Alt+F7. Use macros only where permitted.",
        "InputGroup" => "An atomic group sharing the input queue for mouse, keyboard, clipboard and active window. Connect Body to the first action and end it with Branch end; then Next runs. Example: click a field, then type text. The lock is released on completion, stop, error or timeout (100–60000 ms). Avoid long waits inside. Parallel is not allowed in a group. Individual actions also acquire the queue while they run.",
        "Click" => "Presses and releases the left, right or middle button at the current pointer position, 1–100 times. To hold or drag, use Mouse button Down → Move cursor → Mouse button Up.",
        "MouseButton" => "Presses or releases a mouse button. Kliker Blocks releases buttons it holds when the scenario ends or stops. Corresponds to LDOWN/LUP, RDOWN/RUP and MDOWN/MUP.",
        "Wheel" => "Scrolls the wheel up or down by 1–100 steps. Corresponds to WHEELUP/WHEELDOWN.",
        "Key" => "Briefly presses a key. Supported names include A–Z, 0–9, F1–F24, ENTER, SPACE, TAB, ESC, CTRL, ALT, SHIFT, BACKSPACE, arrows, HOME, END, DELETE, INSERT, PAGEUP, PAGEDOWN and WIN. Corresponds to KEYPRESS.",
        "KeyState" => "Presses or releases a key across blocks, for example CTRL down → A → CTRL up. Kliker Blocks releases keys it still holds on stop. Corresponds to KEYDOWN/KEYUP.",
        "VirtualKey" => "Presses or releases a key by decimal Windows VK code (1–255). Examples: 13=Enter, 17=Ctrl, 18=Alt, 27=Esc, 32=Space, 46=Delete, 65=A, 90=Z, 112=F1, 118=F7. Its name appears below the entered code. Open the searchable VK code table for all codes. OEM keys depend on layout; macro recording can capture passwords.",
        "TypeText" => "Sends Unicode characters to the active window through Windows SendInput; no keyboard-layout change is needed. The target must accept ordinary system input.",
        "ClipboardSet" => "Places text on the Windows clipboard. To paste it, add CTRL down → V → CTRL up blocks.",
        "ClipboardGet" => "Stores clipboard text in a variable, or an empty string if no text is present. Copy in the target application first if needed.",
        "TextFile" => "Works with UTF-8 text files: read, count, replace or insert a numbered line, append, overwrite with one line, delete a line, clear or delete the file. Line numbers start at 1. Insertion past the end appends. Paths are relative to the project folder. Reading/editing is limited to 8 MiB; deletion does not require reading the contents.",
        "TextFileArray" => "TFREADARR reads a UTF-8 file into an array, replacing old entries; TFWRITEARR writes one element per line. Indices start at 0; missing sparse items become 0 in the file. The result variable receives the line count. Limits: 10000 lines and 8 MiB. Elements containing newlines cannot be written.",
        "CsvTable" => "Works with a UTF-8 CSV table without Excel: read or write a cell using 1-based row and column numbers, count rows, or save rows whose chosen cell contains filter text to a separate file. Delimiters are comma, semicolon or TAB; quoted values and embedded newlines are supported. Writing replaces the file atomically; keep a copy of important tables. Limits: 8 MiB, 10000 rows and 256 columns. The result is stored in a variable; relative paths use the project folder. Example: create a two-row data.csv, read row 1 column 2, then log csv_result.",
        "ExcelTable" => "Use .xlsx files to read or write a cell, find the last used row, or find row numbers whose selected cell contains text. Rows and columns start at 1. A blank sheet name chooses the first sheet; writing may create a file or sheet. Writing saves through a temporary file before replacing the original. Input limit: 32 MiB; search examines the first 10000 rows. The result is stored in a variable. Example: write Hello to A1 in workbook.xlsx, then read A1 and log ${excel_result}. Macros and the legacy .xls format are not supported.",
        "PdfText" => "Extract text from one PDF page, all pages or return the page count. Page numbers start at 1; extracting all text is limited to 100 pages. Input is limited to 32 MiB and output to 100000 characters. The result is stored in a variable. Scanned images without a text layer yield no text; use OCR for those. Example: select document.pdf and Page count, then log ${pdf_result}. Password-protected PDFs may need to be opened in another program first.",
        "FileQueue" => "Process files in a folder by mask, sorted by name. Take next stores the full path in a variable and follows File available or Queue empty. Process the file, then use another File queue block to mark it done or failed; set Current file to ${queue_file}. State is stored in the visible JSON file and source files are never deleted. An unfinished file becomes available again after 15 minutes. Maximum 10000 files. Example: take next *.txt, read it, mark done, then return to Take next. Connect a separate error path for failures.",
        "Checkpoint" => "Saves the next block, scalar variables and arrays to a visible JSON file. To resume, open the same graph and choose Scenario → Resume from checkpoint. A checkpoint is rejected if the graph changed; its file is not deleted. File limit: 4 MiB. Open windows, held keys and other external state are not restored. Example: Start → set counter=1 → Checkpoint → log ${counter}; stop after saving and resume from the menu.",
        "IniFile" => "Reads or writes a key in an INI section. A missing section/key reads as an empty string. Writing creates them if needed. The result is placed in a variable.",
        "DirectoryList" => "Lists files or directories matching a wildcard such as *.txt. Supply directory and mask separately, or a path containing * or ? as with GETFILELIST/GETDIRLIST. Array items are names without the directory, indexed from 0. The result variable receives the count. Old array entries are removed. Limit: 10000 entries; no recursive traversal.",
        "HttpGet" => "Performs an HTTP/HTTPS GET and stores the UTF-8 response in a variable. Optional headers are one Name: value per line, up to 32 and 8192 characters total. Fields support ${variable}. Timeout: 100–60000 ms; response limit: 8 MiB. HTTP errors stop the scenario. Use trusted URLs: addresses and headers may contain sensitive data.",
        "HttpPost" => "Sends a UTF-8 POST body to the selected HTTP/HTTPS URL. Choose JSON, plain text or form data. Request limit: 1 MiB; response limit: 8 MiB; timeout: 100–60000 ms. The response and numeric HTTP status go into separate variables, including 4xx/5xx statuses. The body and headers are sent to the selected server, so use a trusted URL. Example: set your own test URL and {\"name\":\"Alex\" as the body, then log ${http_status}.",
        "JsonValue" => "Reads JSON text or ${variable} and extracts a field using a path such as user.name or items[0].id. Strings are returned without quotes; other values keep their JSON representation. Invalid JSON or a missing field stops the scenario. Example: {\"user\":{\"name\":\"Alex\"}} with path user.name returns Alex.",
        "TranslateText" => "Translates phrases and sentences. By default it needs a running LibreTranslate /translate service; localhost:5000 without one fails. A remote service receives the submitted text. For offline use, click Download offline language pair in this block, confirm installation, then select Argos local. Source must be explicit rather than auto; supported codes are ru, en, fr, es, zh and de, subject to installed models. Text supports ${variable} or =expression; output goes to a variable. Limit: 16000 characters, timeout 100–60000 ms.",
        "DetectLanguage" => "Uses LibreTranslate /detect and writes the language code and confidence to two variables. Requires a running server; local Argos alone does not provide this operation. Connection errors stop the scenario.",
        "LanguagePair" => "Checks whether the selected source can reach the target. Choose LibreTranslate to query a running service's /languages, or Argos local to check installed models; the latter needs the optional offline engine. Takes Yes or No. A service connection or local engine error stops the scenario.",
        "TranslateArray" => "Translates up to 500 array strings sequentially through a connected LibreTranslate service by default. Use Download offline language pair to install optional Argos models, then choose Argos local with an explicit source code. The destination array and count update only after success; an error leaves the original result unchanged. Each item has its own timeout; stopping cancels the rest.",
        "Wait" => "Waits 0–3,600,000 milliseconds (WAITMS); stopping interrupts it. This runs only when execution reaches this block. To delay cursor return, connect If cursor moved: Yes → Wait → Move cursor. Wait connected to Repeat: Next runs only after all repetitions.",
        "WaitEvent" => "Waits for a window to open or close by part of its title, or for a file change. A window already in the requested state immediately takes Event. File watching begins when this block runs; earlier changes do not count. A timeout of 100–3,600,000 ms takes Timeout; stopping cancels the wait. Example: when a Notepad window appears, log a message.",
        "UiWait" => "Finds a Windows UI Automation element by AutomationId, name and/or control type. Window title limits the search; a blank title uses the active window. The capture button fills the selector from the element under the pointer after three seconds. Outputs: Found and Timeout. Custom or game interfaces may expose no UI Automation elements; use image search or coordinates there. Example: wait for a test button's AutomationId, then connect Found to an element action.",
        "UiAction" => "Finds the selected element and invokes, enters text, toggles, selects or reads it. Result and error text go into variables; errors take the Error output. Text input requires ValuePattern and invoking requires InvokePattern, so some actions are unavailable on particular controls. Use the capture button to fill the selector. Example: capture a button in a test window, connect Wait for element: Found to this block, then inspect its output in the log.",
        "SetVariable" => "Creates or replaces a variable. Names use A–Z, a–z, 0–9 and _, beginning with a letter or _. Values can be text, a number, ${other_value} or =expression. Names are case-insensitive; the value lasts through the current run.",
        "DefineVariable" => "Creates a variable with an initial value only if it does not exist. Existing values remain unchanged. It is useful for a counter that must persist across scenario passes.",
        "UndefineVariable" => "Deletes the variable and same-named array (UNDEFINE). Reading it afterward yields 0; DEFINE can recreate it. Computed $_ system variables are unaffected.",
        "SetVariableByName" => "SETVAR changes an existing scalar variable using a name supplied as a string, for example $counter or ${reference} containing $counter. Value supports =expression. A missing target is an error; Set variable or DEFINE creates one. Use =getvar($reference) to read indirectly.",
        "MathVariable" => "Adds, subtracts, multiplies or divides a numeric variable. A missing value starts at 0. Operands can use ${other_variable}; fractional values are supported. Division by zero or nonnumeric input reports an error.",
        "ArraySet" => "Writes an array element at index 0–1000000. Names follow the variable-name rule. Index and value can start with = for expressions. Read with =$items[0] or =$items[$i]. A missing item reads as 0.",
        "ArrayAction" => "Appends, pops the last element, gets size, or sorts as text, numbers or automatically. Automatic sorting is numeric only if all elements are numeric. Popping an empty array returns 0. Expressions support arrsize($items) and arrpop($items). A sparse array's size is its highest index plus one. Arrays last for the run.",
        "StringSplit" => "Splits a string on an exact delimiter (STRSEPARATE), replacing the destination array. Indices begin at 0 and empty segments are retained. A count variable receives the number of parts. Delimiter cannot be empty; maximum 10000 parts. String and delimiter support ${variable} or =expression.",
        "IfVariable" => "Tests an existing variable. Equals, not equals and contains compare text case-insensitively; greater/less compare numbers. Comparison input can use ${name}. Branches: Yes and No.",
        "IfExpression" => "Evaluates a field beginning with =. Zero or FALSE takes No; 1 or TRUE takes Yes. For example: =if $counter<10. Expressions support $variable, $array[index], + - * / % ** ^, parentheses, comparisons, &&/and, ||/or, xor, !/not, rnd, sqrt, strlen and other functions. See Conditional expressions and assignment for if/then/else_if/else.",
        "WhileExpression" => "Repeats Body while its =expression is nonzero, reevaluating after each iteration. End Body with Branch end; Next runs when false. The iteration cap prevents accidental endless loops; 0 removes that cap. Stop interrupts the loop.",
        "SwitchValue" => "Compares a value against five options in order and follows the first match. Empty options are skipped; Otherwise runs if none match. Fields accept =expressions. Numbers compare numerically; other text ignores case. Chain Otherwise to another Switch for more options.",
        "IfMoved" => "Checks the cursor distance from its anchor once. Yes means outside the threshold. For a delayed return connect Yes → Wait → Move cursor; for continuous monitoring use Watch cursor.",
        "IfPixel" => "Captures one screen pixel and compares it with a color. Tolerance applies to each R/G/B channel (0 is exact). Yes means a match. The measured color is logged.",
        "WaitPixel" => "Repeatedly checks one pixel until its color matches or a timeout expires. Set the polling interval in milliseconds. Timeout 0 waits until stopped. Outputs are Found and Timeout.",
        "IfKeyDown" => "Checks a key or mouse button. While held yields Yes each pass; On new press yields Yes once per physical press; Toggle changes a stored state on each new press. Repeating scenarios test on each pass. Press-to-assign captures keys or mouse buttons; LMB/RMB/MMB or decimal VK codes can be entered manually. While held corresponds to ISKEYDOWN.",
        "FindColor" => "Finds the first pixel of a given color in a rectangle. Outputs Found/No. Its coordinates are logged and available to Move to found point in the same branch. Corresponds to IF_PIXEL_IN.",
        "FindImage" => "Finds a BMP/PNG template on screen within bounds. Tolerance 0–255 applies per color channel; fully transparent PNG pixels are ignored. Returns the match's top-left corner. Large images/regions can be slow; protected output may not be capturable. Corresponds to IF_PICTURE_IN.",
        "Screenshot" => "Saves a rectangular screen area as PNG, creating the destination folder if needed. The whole rectangle must be inside the virtual desktop.",
        "OcrImage" => "Reads PNG, JPEG, BMP, TIFF, GIF, WebP or PDF locally and stores the result in a variable. PDF pages are rendered in grayscale at 200 DPI and passed to Tesseract. Set PDF page to 1–100 for one page or 0 for all pages (up to 20). GIF and WebP work when the installed Tesseract build includes the matching Leptonica decoders. If the engine or language is missing, the first run offers to download the 64-bit UB Mannheim Tesseract 5.5.3 Windows build from the Tesseract GitHub release (about 24.1 MiB / 25.3 MB) and only the models named in the language field; no network request is made without explicit consent. After consent, Windows UAC asks to approve the installer; confirm the prompt to install Tesseract in LocalAppData\\KLIKER Blocks\\Tesseract-OCR. The app verifies the engine and continues without a restart. If UAC is declined or permission is unavailable, setup stops; you can enter the path to an existing tesseract.exe instead. Engine and file paths containing Cyrillic characters use Windows short names when available. Files are never sent over the network. Automatic setup is unavailable on 32-bit Windows. Limits: 32 MiB PDF/input, 32 MiB rendered page image, 100000 output characters and 1–120 seconds total timeout. Password-protected PDFs cannot be OCRed without a password. Example: set the PDF path to scan.pdf and PDF page to 0; Recognize text reads up to 20 pages into ocr_text, separating page results with a page break. Small or blurry text may be misread.",
        "WaitScreenText" => "Takes a screenshot of the full screen or a configured area at intervals and runs local OCR until the target text appears or the timeout expires. Search text and result support variables. Found continues through the Found output; Timeout handles missing text. Timeout is 1000–600000 ms; each OCR pass is limited to the remaining time and at most 30 seconds. Tesseract and the selected language model must be installed; if missing, the block offers the same explicit-consent setup as Recognize text. Area coordinates use the virtual desktop. Frequent capture increases CPU use, so choose a sensible interval. Example: search for Ready, poll every 500 ms, and connect Found to a message.",
        "ExtractText" => "Extracts a fragment with a .NET regular expression. For example, enter Invoice\\s+#(\\d+) and group 1. Group 0 returns the full match; group 1 returns the first capture. Input supports ${text}; the result is written to the selected variable. No match yields an empty result. A two-second timeout limits pathological expressions. Example: for Invoice #582, use Invoice\\s+#(\\d+) and group 1.",
        "BufferCapture" => "Stores a screen snapshot for later analysis blocks, either the full virtual desktop or a rectangle. Left/top bounds are inclusive; right/bottom are exclusive. A new capture replaces the previous one; repeated checks otherwise see the same pixels. Corresponds to GETSCREEN.",
        "BufferPixel" => "Reads one pixel from the stored snapshot into a variable. Coordinates are screen coordinates. COLORREF is decimal R + G×256 + B×65536; #RRGGBB is also available. Capture screen first.",
        "BufferCount" => "Counts pixels matching a color in an area of the stored snapshot. Tolerance 0–255 applies separately to R/G/B. Writes a result variable. Corresponds to PXLCOUNT. Capture again for fresh pixels.",
        "BufferHash" => "Computes XOR or CRC32 of RGB pixels in a stored-snapshot region and writes an unsigned result. Compare the same region across captures. CRC32 reads R,G,B bytes left-to-right, top-to-bottom.",
        "BufferReplace" => "Changes a color in the stored snapshot, not on the physical screen. Old color -1 means any color. Writes the changed-pixel count. Use Save snapshot to inspect the result. Corresponds to PXLREPLACE.",
        "BufferFindColor" => "Finds the first matching pixel in the stored snapshot. Branches Found/No; coordinates go to Move to found point and $_return1/$_return2. Capture screen first.",
        "BufferFindImage" => "Finds a PNG/BMP/JPG template in the stored snapshot. Writes its top-left coordinates to $_return1/$_return2 for Move to found point. Fully transparent pixels and an optional background color are ignored. Tolerance is separate for R/G/B. Capture first.",
        "BufferScanColor" => "Finds every matching-color pixel in the stored snapshot up to the chosen limit. Replaces an array with X,Y pairs: [0]=X, [1]=Y, [2]=X, [3]=Y, and writes the number of points. Order is top-to-bottom, left-to-right. Corresponds to single-color SCANPXL.",
        "BufferScanImage" => "Finds every template location in the stored snapshot, including overlaps, up to the chosen limit. Replaces an array with X,Y pairs and writes a count. Transparency/background color excludes template pixels. Tolerance 0–255 applies per channel, not as a percentage.",
        "BufferSave" => "Saves the current stored snapshot, including Replace color edits, to a PNG file. It does not capture the screen again. Relative paths start in the saved project's folder.",
        "MoveFound" => "Moves the cursor to the last point found by Find color/image in the same branch, plus an offset. If no point exists, the scenario errors. For a template's center, offset by half its width and height.",
        "IfWindow" => "Finds a visible window by a case-insensitive title substring. Yes if found, otherwise No. With multiple matches, Windows enumeration order determines the first.",
        "ActivateWindow" => "Finds a visible window by title substring, restores it and tries to activate it. Windows may prevent background activation; an elevated target may require Kliker Blocks to run elevated.",
        "MoveWindow" => "Finds a visible window by title substring and sets X/Y and dimensions in screen pixels. Width/height must be positive. Elevated targets may require corresponding permissions.",
        "WindowFind" => "Finds a visible window by title substring or prefix and writes its numeric HWND, or 0 if none. The first match is returned. HWND becomes stale after the window closes.",
        "WindowInfo" => "Reads a window's screen X, Y, width and height from numeric HWND into four variables. An invalid HWND is an error. Corresponds to WNDGETINFO.",
        "WindowAction" => "Acts on numeric HWND: set position, size, full geometry, state (0 minimize, 1 restore), or activate. Windows may reject changes to another process. Corresponds to WNDPOS, WNDSIZE, WNDSETINFO, WNDSTATE and WNDBUMP.",
        "ElementAtPoint" => "Returns the numeric HWND of the Windows element at screen X/Y, or 0 if none. Coordinates accept expressions or the current mouse position. Corresponds to HGET(x,y).",
        "ElementInfo" => "Reads a standard Windows element's text or process PID by HWND into a variable. WM_GETTEXT has a 500 ms timeout and 4096-character cap; custom controls may return no text. Corresponds to HGETTEXT/HGETPID.",
        "ElementSetText" => "Sends WM_SETTEXT to an element by HWND, with a 500 ms timeout and 4096-character cap. Windows may reject cross-process writes; custom controls may not support it. Corresponds to HSETTEXT.",
        "MessageSend" => "Sends a numeric message code (0–65535) to an HWND. Asynchronous POSTMESSAGE returns 1 when queued. Synchronous SENDMESSAGE waits up to 500 ms and writes the result. wParam/lParam accept integers only, not pointers or strings. Avoid unknown codes in other applications.",
        "MessageReceive" => "Reads the last WM_USER (1024) wParam/lParam sent to this Kliker Blocks instance's main window. The new-message flag is 1 once, then 0 until another arrives. Use $_hwnd_self for your window. Instances do not mix messages.",
        "RunFile" => "Launches an .exe, .bat, .cmd, .lnk shortcut, document or associated file through Windows. Click Choose a program or file to search installed Start-menu programs, or Browse files to select any existing file; you can still type a path. Relative paths resolve from the project folder. Arguments are passed as text. Optionally wait for exit with a timeout. If a shortcut launches without returning a process handle, waiting is unavailable. Exit codes, when available, are logged.",
        "RunAhk" => "Runs an .ahk script with AutoHotkey v2. Enter script path and optionally AutoHotkey64.exe/AutoHotkey32.exe v2 path. An empty interpreter field searches standard installation folders. Arguments, wait and timeout are configurable. AHK v1 scripts are unsupported.",
        "RunScenario" => "Runs a saved .klb scenario sequentially: 1–1000 repetitions, 0.1–5.0× speed and up to eight nested levels. The child makes one pass; explicit feedback cycles are rejected. Variables are isolated. In Inputs enter one child_name=value per line, for example child_name=${user_name}. In Outputs enter parent_name=child_name; after a successful child run the value is copied to the parent variable. Names use ASCII letters, digits and underscore. Stopping the parent stops the child.",
        "Repeat" => "Executes Body the specified number of times, then Next once. Next is not an inter-iteration pause: put Wait inside Body and end Body with Branch end. For up to 100 repetitions the journal shows each iteration. Maximum count is 100000.",
        "CallSub" => "Calls a named Subroutine start. On Return from subroutine or an unconnected output, execution continues at this block's Next. Names match case-insensitively; definitions must be unique. Maximum nested call depth is 32.",
        "SubStart" => "Starts a named subroutine. Place it separately from the main chain, choose a unique name, connect Body to its first action and finish with Return from subroutine. Call it through Call subroutine only.",
        "SubReturn" => "Equivalent to END_SUB. Ends the called subroutine and resumes at the caller's Next. It is an error outside a subroutine.",
        "Parallel" => "Starts branches A and B concurrently, waits for both, then follows Next. End each branch with Branch end. Input actions are queued to prevent mixed simultaneous keys/clicks.",
        "BranchEnd" => "Ends the current repeat or parallel branch. It also ends execution in an ordinary chain.",
        "Log" => "Writes text to the execution journal. If Log file ran earlier, it appends that text to its UTF-8 file. Full journal-to-file recording is a separate Scenario setting. Log actions also records execution of this block.",
        "LogFile" => "Duplicates later PRINT/LOGWRITE and Message block output to a UTF-8 file in the project folder. Enter only a filename, up to 160 characters. The file is created when this block runs, even if no message follows; its full path appears in the journal. Empty disables output. Appends across runs; limit 8 MiB. LOGCLEAR does not clear this file.",
        "LogClear" => "Clears the visible built-in journal. With no UI attached, continues without effect. Corresponds to LOGCLEAR().",
        "LogShow" => "Shows or hides the built-in journal panel: 1 show, 0 hide. The Scenario menu can open it manually. Separate LOGSHOW window coordinates are not supported.",
        "PlaySound" => "Starts a WAV file asynchronously and continues. A relative path is resolved against the project folder. Missing or invalid WAV files report errors. Corresponds to SOUND(file).",
        "SpeakText" => "Speaks text with modern installed Windows voices. Text supports ${variable} and =expression, so it can read results from earlier blocks. Leave Voice blank for automatic selection by text language (ru for Cyrillic, zh for Chinese); the picker lists voice languages. Selecting a voice of the wrong language reports an error instead of silent speech. Rate is -10 to 10, volume 0–100, text at most 16000 characters. The block waits for speech to finish; stopping the scenario interrupts playback. Put it in a parallel branch for concurrent work. Voices depend on Windows; speech does not translate text.",
        "Beep" => "Plays a system tone at 37–32767 Hz for 1–5000 ms, then continues. If the old Windows Beep API is unsupported, playback falls back to the normal audio device. If both fail, the error is logged. Corresponds to BEEP(freq,time).",
        "InputDialog" => "Shows a text prompt and saves the answer to a variable. The default is returned if time expires before input starts; timeout 0 waits indefinitely. Cancel returns an empty string. Timeout is 0–3600 seconds, default 5. Stopping closes the dialog. Corresponds to INPUTBOX(message,default[,delay]). Set the title, X/Y and size in this block. Leave both X and Y blank to center the dialog; enter both for an exact position. Blank width or height grows to fit the text, starting at 435×205. Explicit width is 360–3000 and height 185–3000 pixels. X/Y are screen coordinates, not graph coordinates.",
        "ChoiceDialog" => "Shows a question. Choose the visible button set by name: 0 OK; 1 OK/Cancel; 2 Abort/Retry/Ignore; 3 Yes/No/Cancel; 4 Yes/No; 5 Retry/Cancel. A set number is NOT a result code: Yes returns 6 and No returns 7. The selected set's result codes appear under the field. The icon is also chosen by name. Stopping closes the dialog. Corresponds to DIALOGBOX(message,buttons[,icon]). Title, X/Y and size are set in this block. Leave both X/Y blank to center on screen. Blank dimensions grow to fit the question, starting at 470×190. Explicit width is 360–3000 and height 180–3000 pixels. X/Y are screen coordinates, not graph coordinates.",
        "RadioDialog" => @"Shows one choice per line (1–100 choices, up to 250 characters each). Press Enter between choices in the block properties. In graph code, those line breaks appear as \n within one value line; the properties show separate choices. A template \& in properties appears as \\& in code because the code also escapes the backslash. Each line may contain a template such as =[(${counter}+2)*3]& units \&: with counter=1 it displays ‘9 units &’. OK returns the chosen 1-based index; cancel or OK without a selection returns 0. Stopping closes the dialog. Set title, X/Y and size in the block. Leave both X/Y blank to center on screen. Blank dimensions grow to fit the question and choices, starting at 465×410. Explicit width is 360–3000 and height 260–3000 pixels. X/Y are screen coordinates, not graph coordinates.",
        "HintPopup" => "Requests a Windows notification with message and title, then continues. It uses the Kliker Blocks tray icon; Windows settings may hide or delay it. Corresponds to HINTPOPUP(message[,title]).",
        "Stop" => "Stops the whole scenario, including parallel branches after their current action. External processes already started remain running.",
        _ => null
    };
}
